[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Strongly see dance 2.0 is so much more than just dialing in the perfect prompt. In this video I\'m going to be showing you what systems I\'m using in [music] order to control see dance 2.0 and what I believe will be crucial when see dance 2.05 [music]'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'comes out. The see dance 2.5 is almost here and it\'s like Christmas. Everyone\'s just so excited about the fact that you can use so many references. So now the problem is that everyone is still trying to dial in that perfect prompt and the problem with the perfect prompt is that'

[00:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it\'s an illusion. There\'s no such thing as the perfect prompt because really see dance 2.0 uses the very first maybe 30 tokens and then the rest of it is just fluff. And if you use a long enough prompt, you tend to just confuse see'

[00:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dance 2.0. So a lot of people that are against AI seem to believe that the only thing you need is a prompt and that just creates everything for you. Now anyone who\'s actually used AI generators, especially'

[00:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'AI video generators, for more than 5 minutes will attest to the fact that that\'s not how it works. If you\'re a visual effects artist, it drives you insane because you have absolutely no control. Coming from a place like let\'s'

[01:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'say 3D animation where you had all the control in the world and that\'s a big problem if you\'re trying to create cohesion, like a story that actually progresses properly as opposed to just being a whole bunch of different videos'

[01:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that you have to kind of cobble together. Recently you\'ve probably been seeing on social media people using 3D animatics in order to control see dance 2.0 and get much more control over the objects in the actual shot and how they'

[01:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'move and translate and everything like that. Now I thought to myself, I\'ve got blender experience. I\'ve been using blender for well over 10 years. And as you probably saw in one of my earlier videos, I actually used blender to control see dance when it initially came'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'out and drive the van through the canyon. Now the issue that I have is I\'m not exactly a character animator. But when it comes to actually animating a character and doing like complex moves,'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I prefer to download things from Mixamo and other motion capture libraries. You know, over the last couple of weeks using Cloud, I\'ve actually been able to create an add-on for Blender that will automate basically all the animation process. It'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will allow you to sequence different motion capture libraries and then that way you are able to actually create animations that go from one motion capture to the other without it jumping'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all over the screen. The other great thing is that it actually automates the process of creating a open pose for your character. So, then that way we\'re able to actually get an open pose that matches our character properly and'

[02:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'overlays it with the actual 3D image. Now, the great thing about this is it gives us a much more control in the final image. SeeDance 2.0 is able to read open pose. It can look at our 3D'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'video and actually take the camera information, take the actual object information, and in this case it\'s going to be a character flying around, and create exactly what it is that we want. All you need is a character from Mixamo'

[03:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or you can create your own as long as it\'s got the exact same armature system as Mixamo, and it\'ll create a rig that works with this add-on. Now, the great thing about this is the quality that comes out of Blender'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'doesn\'t have to be that great. So, you\'re not having to worry about ray tracing and all of that. So, it renders out pretty quick. So, you can actually create these animations relatively quick. Now, with this one specifically, I put this all together in roughly about'

[03:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '10-15 minutes, rendered it out, and then actually sent it up to SeeDance in order to get my video back. And if you\'re interested in using this add-on, it\'s going to be in my Patreon. So, there\'s going to be a link in the description.'

[03:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you like this video so far, let me know in the comments down below what it is that you have been creating with SeeDance 2.0 or what some misconceptions that you\'ve actually had with prompting Ciness 2.0 and how you\'ve actually been'

[03:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'able to rectify it. And like honestly, the prompts aren\'t that important. It\'s what you give it. Now, it\'s kind of like when you\'re filmmaking, you have all this reference material before you even'

[04:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get onto the set to start filming. You have the colorist grading looks for you so then that way everything can actually match. You have the art directors and the set designers. All of these things are coming together before you actually start filming. Now, if you go into'

[04:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Ciness 2.0 or Ciness 2.5 eventually with nothing, like a blank slate, it\'s going to create whatever it wants. Most anti-AI people are under the impression'

[04:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that all you need is a prompt and it\'ll create everything that you need in the world. That\'s not how it works. AI doesn\'t work like that at all and it\'s a very big misconception. It\'s actually something that was happening in the \'90s'

[04:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when Photoshop first came out. A lot of people in the film industry, comic book industry were under the impression that all you need to do is press a button and Photoshop would do everything for you. And we know full well that that is not'

[04:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the case at all. Really, we\'re here to make movies. That\'s all I care about. Honestly, the only thing I care about is telling stories. That should be the only thing you care about. The technology that you use in order to create that'

[05:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'movie shouldn\'t matter. These things like yeah, the way that it was created was kind of in a dodgy way and a lot of people are like, "Oh, we\'re making, you know, these'

[05:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
